# Every resolved `between` the emitter draws as an attribution. One row per row of
# eliminations/resolved.sqlc: the composed layer, and the layer of another document the double
# counting runs against, with the notation that document is imported by.
#
# The relationship and nothing wider
#   examples/diagramming/main.rs draws one `relationship` per row, from the composed layer to the
#   layer it names, and imports the notation it needs. Which quantity the elimination is against,
#   and which `between` it was, stay in eliminations/resolved.sqlc: the quantity is
#   `pm.summed_quantity`, which sqlx has no mapping for, and a program with no use for a quantity is
#   better unable to read one, as diagrams/calls.sqlc argues for magnitudes.
#
# A bag, as its source is
#   One row per eliminated quantity and `between`, so an elimination against two quantities that
#   runs against one layer is drawn once per quantity.
#
# Defined, not read
#   A program reads these columns, so this statement defines its vocabulary in its own
#   WITH clause: a view keeps the rows and loses the column's origin, which is where
#   NOT NULL lives. assets/sqlc/views.sqlc says what a definition is.
#
-- eliminations/resolved.sqlc projected to the two ends of the relationship.
WITH
notations AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/notations.sqlc)
),
eliminations_between AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(eliminations/between.sqlc)
),
eliminations_references AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(eliminations/references.sqlc)
),
resolved AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(eliminations/resolved.sqlc)
)
SELECT b.composition, b.composed_layer, b.notation, b.resolved_filing, b.layer
FROM (
    :compose(eliminations/resolved.sqlc)
) b
